{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"ecosystem/inference-engines","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Inference Engines","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Say what an inference engine does that a provider hides from you","Give the three real reasons to run a model yourself","Pick an engine for one machine, for a GPU server, or for a cluster","Explain why batching decides throughput","Put a chat interface in front of a local model"]},"prerequisites":[{"key":"ecosystem/inference-providers","number":36,"title":"Inference Providers","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"ecosystem/inference-providers","number":36,"title":"Inference Providers","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"ecosystem/ui-design","number":38,"title":"UI Design","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"why-you-would-run-it-yourself","text":"Why You Would Run it Yourself","depth":2},{"id":"the-engines-by-the-size-of-the-problem","text":"The Engines, by the Size of the Problem","depth":2},{"id":"the-one-idea-that-explains-the-numbers","text":"The One Idea That Explains the Numbers","depth":2},{"id":"something-to-put-in-front-of-it","text":"Something to Put in Front of it","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"️-tools","text":"🛠️ Tools","depth":3},{"id":"️-repositories","text":"🗃️ Repositories","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" named the engines. Then it said that you rarely touch them directly. That is true, and it is why that module moved straight on to Ollama. This module is about the times when you do touch them."}],"lead":true},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"inference engine"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the program that loads the weights and turns your tokens into the next token. It has these jobs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It manages GPU memory."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It batches requests together."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It caches attention state between turns."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It decides what runs in what order."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An inference provider, the subject of "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"ecosystem/inference-providers","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Inference Providers"}]},{"type":"text","text":", is somebody else who runs one of these engines for you and charges you per token."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-you-would-run-it-yourself","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why You Would Run it Yourself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"There are three reasons, and only these three are worth the trouble."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The data cannot leave."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Examples are health records, legal documents, and anything under a rule that says the text must stay on your infrastructure. This is the most common reason, much more common than the other two. If it applies to you, it decides the question on its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The volume makes it cheaper."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Paying per token is a very good deal at first, but it stops being one when your volume grows large enough. There is a crossover point. Past that point, a rented GPU that runs all day costs less than the same work bought by the token. If you are past it, the arithmetic is not close: the difference is large."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You need something a provider will not give you."}]},{"type":"text","text":" For example:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a fine-tuned model of your own, from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training LLMs"}]}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a model that nobody hosts"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"an unusual quantization"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"control over the exact version, so that an upgrade never surprises you"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If none of those is true, use a provider. Self-hosting is a real operational job. In the first month, you mostly learn what you did not budget for."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-engines-by-the-size-of-the-problem","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The Engines, by the Size of the Problem"}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"37.1","title":"The Engines, by the Size of the Problem","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/dcae5d7559a1cd6cf0aade0d8cff94fe.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/e43e91825d0cd607585e02c794b09d95.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"On your own machine"}]},{"type":"text","text":", use the wrappers. "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covered "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://ollama.com/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Ollama"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://lmstudio.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LM Studio"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and they are the right answer here. They hide the engine, and they get you running in minutes. Underneath, both of them are built on "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/llamacpp.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"llama.cpp"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":". It is LLM inference written in C and C++. It is also the reason that a quantized model can run on a laptop at all. Use llama.cpp directly in two cases: when you want a build flag that the wrapper does not expose, or when you are putting a model on hardware that nobody else supports."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"On a GPU server with real users"}]},{"type":"text","text":", the wrappers are no longer enough. Here you want a serving engine."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/vllm.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"vLLM"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the usual default. It supports a broad range of hardware. Its continuous batching is what gives it good throughput."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/sglang.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"SGLang"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a high-performance serving framework for language and multimodal models. It is strong when requests share a long common prefix. If every request starts with the same big system prompt, that is exactly your case."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/nvidia.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"TensorRT-LLM"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" comes from NVIDIA. It gets the most out of NVIDIA hardware specifically. You compile the model into an optimised engine ahead of time. That costs you a build step, and it gives you lower latency."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Across many machines"}]},{"type":"text","text":", the problem is no longer how to run one server program well. The problem becomes how to coordinate a whole cluster of machines. That is what "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/llm-d/llm-d"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/llm-d.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"llm-d"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is for. It runs inference on Kubernetes with modern accelerators. The platform then handles scheduling and routing for you, which means that it decides which machine runs each request. It also does cache-aware placement: it prefers a machine that already holds the needed data in its cache."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-one-idea-that-explains-the-numbers","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The One Idea That Explains the Numbers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you remember only one mechanism from this module, remember "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"batching"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A GPU that runs one request at a time is mostly idle. The reason is that generating a token takes a small amount of arithmetic and a huge amount of memory traffic. If you run many requests together, the GPU reads the weights from memory once and uses them for every request in the group. That is why people measure throughput on these engines with many concurrent users. It is also why the same hardware can look brilliant or hopeless. It depends only on how full the batch is."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Continuous batching is the improvement that matters in practice. The engine does not wait for a batch to finish. Instead, it puts new requests into the batch as older ones complete, so the GPU is never left without work. This is also why your own benchmark, with one request at a time, will badly mislead you about what the server can do."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"And batching connects to "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"intermediate/context-engineering","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context Engineering"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from the other side. The attention state for a long context has to stay in GPU memory. So a full context window is slower to reason over. It also takes up memory that another user's request needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"something-to-put-in-front-of-it","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Something to Put in Front of it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An engine gives you an API and nothing to look at. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/open-webui-mono.svg","alt":"","mono":true},{"type":"text","text":"Open WebUI"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the interface that people put in front of an engine. It is a chat UI that talks to Ollama, or to anything that uses the same API shape as OpenAI. It has accounts, conversation history and model switching. Some teams want a private ChatGPT on their own hardware. For them, that is the whole stack, in two pieces: the engine and Open WebUI."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An inference engine is the program that actually runs the weights. It takes care of:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"GPU memory"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"batching"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the attention cache"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"scheduling"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A provider is somebody else who operates an engine for you."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Run your own engine for one of three reasons:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The data cannot leave your infrastructure."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The volume has passed the point where renting a GPU is cheaper than buying tokens."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You need a model or a version that nobody hosts."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Otherwise, use a provider, because self-hosting is an operational job."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Match the engine to the size of the problem. On your own machine, use Ollama or LM Studio, with llama.cpp underneath. On a GPU server with real users, use vLLM, SGLang or TensorRT-LLM. SGLang suits a shared long prefix, and TensorRT-LLM gets the most out of NVIDIA hardware. When the problem becomes a cluster, use llm-d."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Batching is the idea that explains every number in this module. A GPU that serves one request is mostly idle. Continuous batching keeps it full. That is why a benchmark with one request at a time tells you almost nothing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-tools","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🛠️ Tools"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://ollama.com/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Ollama"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://lmstudio.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LM Studio"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the local wrappers, covered in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-repositories","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🗃️ Repositories"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"llama.cpp"}]},{"type":"text","text":": LLM inference in C and C++, and the base that the local wrappers are built on"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"SGLang"}]},{"type":"text","text":": high-performance serving, strong when requests share a prefix"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"TensorRT-LLM"}]},{"type":"text","text":": from NVIDIA, compiled ahead of time for lower latency"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/llm-d/llm-d"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"llm-d"}]},{"type":"text","text":": serving across many machines on Kubernetes"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Open WebUI"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the chat interface to put in front of any of them"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"What does an inference engine do?","options":["It trains the weights of a model on new data.","It shows a chat interface where users talk to a model.","It loads the weights and produces the next token.","It sells access to a model and charges per token."],"answer":2,"explain":"An inference engine loads the weights, turns tokens into the next token, and manages GPU memory and batching. Charging per token is what a provider does when it runs an engine for you."},{"question":"The module gives three reasons to run a model yourself. Which reason is the most common?","options":["The data must stay on your own infrastructure.","Self-hosting takes less work than a provider.","Per-token pricing always costs more than a GPU.","Your own GPU always answers faster than a provider."],"answer":0,"explain":"Data that must stay on your own infrastructure is the most common reason, and it settles the question alone. Cost is a reason only after you pass a crossover point, not always."},{"question":"Your team serves many users from one GPU server. Every request starts with the same long system prompt. Which engine fits this case best?","options":["Ollama","SGLang","llm-d","llama.cpp"],"answer":1,"explain":"SGLang is a serving engine that is strong when requests share a long common prefix. Ollama and llama.cpp are for one machine, and llm-d is for many machines."},{"question":"You test your self-hosted model with one request at a time. Why does this test mislead you about the server?","options":["Generating a token is mostly arithmetic, so one request fills the GPU.","The engine waits for a full batch before it starts any request.","Batching only matters when you serve a model across many machines.","A GPU with one request is mostly idle, and the batch decides throughput."],"answer":3,"explain":"Generating a token needs little arithmetic and a lot of memory traffic, so one request leaves the GPU mostly idle. When many requests run together, one weight loading serves all of them."},{"question":"An engine gives you an API and nothing to look at. What is Open WebUI?","options":["A serving engine that batches many requests on one GPU.","A chat interface with accounts, history and model switching.","A tool that compiles a model ahead of time for NVIDIA.","A platform that runs inference across many Kubernetes machines."],"answer":1,"explain":"Open WebUI is a chat interface that talks to Ollama or to any engine with the OpenAI API shape. The other options describe engines: vLLM, TensorRT-LLM and llm-d."}]}